  ],
  "seoTitle": "2:1 Multiplexer Using Logic Gates: Circuit and Truth Table",
  "seoDescription": "Implement a 2:1 multiplexer from an inverter, two AND gates and an OR gate. Truth table, boolean expression and why muxes matter.",
  "keywords": [
    "2 to 1 multiplexer",
    "multiplexer using logic gates",
    "mux implementation",
    "2:1 mux circuit"
  ],
  "authorName": "Nandscape",
  "publishedAt": "2026-08-15T14:28:40.367Z",
  "body": [
    {
      "id": "p-intro",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "text": "A "
        },
        {
          "text": "2:1 multiplexer",
          "marks": [
            {
              "kind": "bold"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": " picks one of two inputs and forwards it to a single output. A select line decides which. It is the hardware equivalent of a ternary expression: "
        },
        {
          "text": "Y = SEL ? B : A",
          "marks": [
            {
              "kind": "code"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "h-truth",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "text": "Truth table"
    },
    {
      "id": "t-truth",
      "type": "table",
      "headers": [
        "SEL",
        "Y"
      ],
      "rows": [
        [
          "0",
          "A"
        ],
        [
          "1",
          "B"
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "p-truth",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "text": "Written out over all three inputs it is eight rows, but the behaviour compresses to those two lines: SEL chooses the source, and the unselected input has no effect on the output at all."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "h-expression",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "text": "The boolean expression"
    },
    {
      "id": "p-expression",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "text": "Y = (A AND NOT SEL) OR (B AND SEL)",
          "marks": [
            {
              "kind": "code"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "h-gates",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "text": "Step-by-step construction"
    },
    {
      "id": "h-inv",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "text": "Invert the select line"
    },
    {
      "id": "p-inv",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "text": "You need both SEL and NOT SEL, so start with a single inverter."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "h-and",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "text": "Two AND gates act as gates in the literal sense"
    },
    {
      "id": "p-and",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "text": "AND A with NOT SEL, and B with SEL. Exactly one of these can be non-zero at a time, so one branch is always forced to 0 while the other passes its input straight through."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "h-or",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 3,
      "text": "OR the branches"
    },
    {
      "id": "p-or",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "text": "Since one branch is always 0, ORing them just forwards whichever branch is live. That is the output."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "h-why",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "text": "Why multiplexers matter"
    },
    {
      "id": "p-why",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "text": "Selection is everywhere in hardware: choosing which register feeds an ALU, which result to write back, which instruction to run next. A multiplexer is also functionally complete on its own, which is why "
        },
        {
          "text": "lookup-table based FPGAs",
          "marks": [
            {
              "kind": "link",
              "href": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Field-programmable_gate_array"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": " can implement arbitrary logic from little more than muxes and memory."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "h-build",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "text": "Build it yourself"
    },
    {
      "id": "p-build",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "text": "Four gates. Then try building a 4:1 mux from three 2:1 muxes, which is the standard way to scale it."
        }
      ]
    },
